<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one. Use the prime-indexed subset roots from Node four, and write c sub S, d sub S, and g sub S for their evaluations. The preceding steps prove R equals W equals one. Unpack R as the alternating product of d sub S over c sub S, and W as the alternating product of g sub S over c sub S. Even subsets contribute positive first powers, and odd subsets contribute negative first powers. Every denominator is a unit in our evaluation ring. Moving the inverse factors across gives an exact equality between the even and odd products.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, there are only two inputs, one and omega. The d over c ratio at one equals that ratio at omega, and the same is true for g over c. Define lambda as c of omega divided by c of one. Both values have magnitude three, so lambda has magnitude one. Rearrange the two ratio equalities: d of omega is lambda times d of one, and g of omega is lambda times g of one. The same multiplier also carries c of one to c of omega. This forward multiplier is the inverse of Delta of c.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The three values share a rotation. They need not be the same point: the rotation preserves their relative positions. Now recall the exact integral combination E of P: d of P minus c of P, divided by two, plus g of P minus c of P, divided by one plus i. These polynomial quotients were formed before reduction. Substitute the three shared-rotation identities. Factor lambda from both differences. The result is E of omega equals lambda times E of one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Divide this last identity by c of omega, which equals lambda times c of one. The common multiplier cancels, leaving E of omega over c of omega equal to E of one over c of one. We divide by the nonzero unit values of c, never by E of one. Thus the argument also covers E of one equal to zero. The E arrow need not lie on the radius-three circle.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Here is a numerical schematic, not a construction satisfying all Hadamard conditions. Take c of one equal to three, d of one equal to negative three, and g of one equal to three i. Rotate all three by lambda equal to omega. The combination gives E of one equal to negative three plus three i. After dividing by the corresponding c value, both normalized E values are negative one plus i. The two normalized arrows coincide.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The raw E values need not be equal. They differ by lambda, which may also have a nontrivial residue. Equality after division by c does not assert equality of their raw residues. We have assigned no all-ones residue to the schematic values.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For several distinct prime divisors of u, retain the full subset-product identities. We cannot infer one common rotation between every pair of inputs. Keep two outputs separate: the normalized equality for the two-input case, and the general exact products. Node seven uses the normalized equality for the guiding example, and product expansion for the general argument.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
